-aef0-4ddc-82b7-15d285787b98" class="r-1ftr143" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="94ef11a9-a00d-409f-a3d9-55952f7cb1e6" aria-controls="replo-:Rfr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1knsy0e"><div data-rid="a0c63869-2f5e-4e06-b203-abc616f2c611" class="r-365ic4 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h3>What is creatine, and how does it work?</h3></span></div><div data-rid="233011f0-d159-4a13-9a63-f28a79f049b7" class="r-akeucq"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-7mb0sa" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:Rfr2:"><div data-rid="7ba5d6a0-3f52-47a2-8afa-2377d8211852" class="r-npos18"><div data-rid="78209860-0d23-4bcf-9159-c411dd0deffa" class="r-11tafzw alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Creatine is a natural substance found in muscles that helps provide energy during high-intensity activities. It works by increasing the production of ATP (adenosine triphosphate), which is the primary energy source for muscle contractions.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="268621f9-533d-4858-b4ce-15fd4f5de252" class="r-mid3er"></div><div data-rid="6f9d6249-67b9-4e1f-b1bd-3a5f2e152147" class="r-1a6wstf" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="1b3ca638-ef12-4f10-ab2d-557637caa138" aria-controls="replo-:Rvr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1ja36g1"><div data-rid="48c6fa08-cb4c-4c3a-8555-a6b8dd05263e" class="r-u49khw alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h3>Is Creatine safe to use?</h3></span></div><div data-rid="d15bb09d-e53a-463e-980e-018df9a5a806" class="r-1uehqa8"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1i6bfvh" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:Rvr2:"><div data-rid="2836655e-3b4b-4a31-9c65-48e4d8e060dc" class="r-mxoav4"><div data-rid="54642e36-b125-4a32-a101-02d97592cf67" class="r-16w1z3n alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Yes, creatine is one of the most researched supplements and has been proven  to be safe for most people when used as directed. Always stick to the  recommended dosages and consult with a healthcare professional if you have any underlying health conditions like kidney infection.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="89528a95-79f4-4c27-a96c-9ff28deb3f81" class="r-18qupp1"></div><div data-rid="6714cc40-669e-449c-b445-de8060e07828" class="r-1fzunh9" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="c26f5fa6-45cd-455c-83e5-94c8871f1da6" aria-controls="replo-:R1fr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1bidx8a"><div data-rid="fcb00baf-73c7-48d0-aaf1-60dc370889a8" class="r-m91lv2 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Is Creatine only for bodybuilders and athletes?</p></span></div><div data-rid="3deb9cdb-12dd-49d9-abec-150098ff6e0b" class="r-1y2lrgx"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-4enzjj" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R1fr2:"><div data-rid="b3b4fb25-12ce-495f-8612-48d4024eb1bd" class="r-hunlj0"><div data-rid="26941d13-5f4c-4776-b04f-fb7d2dd891af" class="r-19xbv4k alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>While creatine is popular among bodybuilders and athletes due to its performance-enhancing effects, it can also benefit individuals engaged in various forms of exercise, including resistance training, high-intensity interval training, and endurance activities.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="95b07820-d43c-41f5-9dcd-0f61d39cf7a5" class="r-1fzi9ci"></div><div data-rid="eb5c654c-561e-41c9-97a5-bc76eed9b5e3" class="r-zd1wf9" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="b8281628-7586-449a-bdee-ff6461ccceee" aria-controls="replo-:R1vr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-cwvju"><div data-rid="9fb7e9b7-1c28-4d1e-8b84-c1956d2eca3b" class="r-1d4rdrd alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>How best should I take Creatine for the best results?</p></span></div><div data-rid="d1cb396e-fc49-4f0d-9be8-b1cecfca93fa" class="r-1m6iged"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-6ztq5d" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R1vr2:"><div data-rid="44b1f680-40e4-4bb2-96f6-42b88b6c9663" class="r-z69fh4"><div data-rid="f19194d2-5de3-4c6d-86bb-7ba4dc3184fd" class="r-e1bvpj alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>A common dosing strategy is to take 3-5 grams of creatine monohydrate daily preferably after a workout for maximum  results.  </p><p>You can also take a higher dose(20 grams) for 5-6 days to saturate muscle stores very quickly followed by a maintenance dose.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="9154088a-444b-483d-884d-a9c7c6be4214" class="r-1ueoju7"></div><div data-rid="be2b4cef-1101-4207-a293-236b85291ca9" class="r-1sv7c59" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="e7d235d6-bced-458b-b9a3-660a57875ae1" aria-controls="replo-:R2fr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-11lnn9v"><div data-rid="f2d3c61e-f702-46ed-ad87-5caad2bfeb20" class="r-4aq3gw alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Can I take Creatine daily?</p></span></div><div data-rid="10637b06-063c-4cfa-b0b1-5f46eda2105c" class="r-nb1g8d"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1qxtu3h" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R2fr2:"><div data-rid="98bb1657-a8e1-457d-b38e-31951c972fdb" class="r-15hg6p9"><div data-rid="eef5e856-91a6-44c0-8d0a-b0b047c49cb2" class="r-15gujtg alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Yes, you can take creatine daily. In fact, for most individuals, taking creatine daily is the standard recommendation to maintain muscle creatine stores at optimal levels.</p><p> Taking creatine daily ensures that your muscles remain saturated with creatine, maxing the potential for muscle gains and recovery.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="3264b163-4ad1-4299-9ce7-756b57e92958" class="r-y61y78"></div><div data-rid="2743d786-ff7d-4b84-8efd-000c4507a72c" class="r-y7ski5" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="ffd8d619-f68f-4b36-b7c8-6f737dc902c3" aria-controls="replo-:R2vr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1u7vb7s"><div data-rid="250b79fa-0a9b-431c-9676-11487b1b367d" class="r-y4walf alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Will creatine make me gain weight or look bloated?</p></span></div><div data-rid="bf53e24e-33fa-48ed-a836-0ea190b3ad14" class="r-2e7uro"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1mok8nl" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R2vr2:"><div data-rid="51acc5f1-197e-426a-b967-71ea45edb8a9" class="r-enaajr"><div data-rid="64db6b1f-cefa-4821-90e0-b7cb6e378523" class="r-1dsmyef alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>While creatine can cause slight water retention in some individuals, it primarily leads to increased muscle mass rather than fat gain. Any initial weight gain is typically due to water retention within the muscles, not bloating. With proper hydration, this effect is usually temporary and will subside over time.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="d1ac34ea-8da4-4fcd-b689-bb95e6c49ef2" class="r-1wcyuuc"></div><div data-rid="f5d2c477-f51f-4a75-b0c3-b40e0b6dc2f7" class="r-1554p4t" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="d73f9f53-df45-425e-ac36-92fb3d1435b5" aria-controls="replo-:R3fr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1alerhs"><div data-rid="50a467d7-5869-4aa9-92a3-807aa6460d34" class="r-1bo8pf4 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Can women use creatine, or is it just for men?</p></span></div><div data-rid="f9377c5c-3565-4b98-908b-45c6be274967" class="r-110nea1"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-83sx0u" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R3fr2:"><div data-rid="b05b4bdc-6e65-4659-a6c1-5098c7499ffe" class="r-gbn2oi"><div data-rid="0a97151d-da85-4c85-82c3-70f6e2bd25c3" class="r-7t9z83 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Creatine can be beneficial for both men and women looking to improve their  fitness performance or increase muscle mass. There is no evidence to suggest that it is exclusive to men, and many women athletes use creatine as part of their training regimen.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="407bc26b-a9d2-4a5e-b35a-64dd51cdc070" class="r-74cvex"></div><div data-rid="b599aa17-03b4-48ef-9260-86d1542daf42" class="r-yr4flx" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="597ec1ce-9e93-46eb-ad5e-54ed691e1bf9" aria-controls="replo-:R3vr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-mt55bp"><div data-rid="0cb742bb-e475-49d1-8c89-f5f54daa73ca" class="r-1g960zw alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Does Creatine cause Acne?</p></span></div><div data-rid="1cb41926-c813-4726-8b1d-e7d7642df485" class="r-1hnwxps"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1dfiva1" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R3vr2:"><div data-rid="4d21c2c5-eb61-4dcc-a775-3a21933e66fa" class="r-qe2mq4"><div data-rid="9e8d3699-6617-4f0b-95d0-7b177dcd5571" class="r-s9fzst alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>There is no proven evidence that creatine causes acne. Infact, creatine may have some benefits for your skin, especially in helping to fight aging.</p><p>Additionally, there is some evidence that creatine can help fight wrinkles or  sagging and sun damaged skin.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="1871bdd4-4eec-4d8b-836c-70cf15f4ba6b" class="r-1ipwj06"></div></div></div></div></div><style>@import url(https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@100;200;300;400;500;600;700;800;900;950)</style></div>
